--- title Words, contexts and binding
--- about What a word is bound to is not part of how it was written, so
--- about these are the natives that ask about a binding or change one.
--- about The pinned boundaries are mostly about what counts as a word
--- about worth collecting and what nesting does.

--- id contexts/collecting-the-words-in-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Unique, and in the order first seen.
--- code
mold collect-words [a b a c]
--- result
"[a b c]"

--- id contexts/collecting-from-an-empty-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold collect-words []
--- result
"[]"

--- id contexts/a-set-word-is-collected-as-a-plain-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold collect-words [a: 1 b]
--- result
"[a b]"

--- id contexts/collecting-only-the-set-words
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold collect-words/set [a: 1 b]
--- result
"[a]"

--- id contexts/nested-blocks-are-not-looked-into
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the boundary /deep exists to cross.
--- code
mold collect-words [a [b]]
--- result
"[a]"

--- id contexts/looking-into-nested-blocks
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold collect-words/deep [a [b]]
--- result
"[a b]"

--- id contexts/a-fresh-block-has-no-line-break
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2]
new-line? b
--- result
#(false)

--- id contexts/marking-a-line-break
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The marker is a property of the position rather than a value
--- note in the block, which is why it takes its own pair of natives.
--- code
b: [1 2]
new-line b true
new-line? b
--- result
#(true)

--- id contexts/evaluating-a-block-inside-an-object
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note WITH binds the block to the object, so a bare word reaches the
--- note object's field rather than anything outside.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
with o [a]
--- result
1

--- id contexts/assigning-inside-an-object
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And the assignment lands in the object, not in a local.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
with o [a: 2 a]
--- result
2

--- id contexts/an-object-made-the-short-way
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note OBJECT is MAKE OBJECT! with the datatype left out.
--- code
o: object [a: 1]
o/a
--- result
1

--- id contexts/an-ordinary-object-holds-self
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So SELFLESS? is false for one, and the question is only
--- note interesting for the contexts that do not.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
selfless? o
--- result
#(false)

--- id contexts/an-ordinary-word-is-not-protected
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
protected? 'foo
--- result
#(false)

--- id contexts/an-ordinary-block-is-not-protected
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1]
protected? b
--- result
#(false)

--- id contexts/unbinding-a-word-answers-the-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
unbind 'a
--- result
a

--- id contexts/unbinding-a-block-answers-the-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [a]
mold unbind b
--- result
"[a]"

--- id contexts/binding-a-word-that-the-context-has
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
mold bind 'a o
--- result
"a"

--- id contexts/binding-a-word-the-context-has-not-got
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note /new adds it rather than leaving it loose, which is how a
--- note generated word is given somewhere to live. base-defs.reb uses
--- note exactly this to define SPEC-OF and its five siblings.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
mold bind/new 'zz o
--- result
"zz"

--- id contexts/a-word-bound-with-new-can-be-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
w: bind/new 'zz o
set w 9
get w
--- result
9

--- id contexts/binding-a-block-answers-the-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
mold bind [a] o
--- result
"[a]"

--- id contexts/binding-to-a-word-uses-that-words-context
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The target may be a word rather than an object, and then it
--- note means "wherever this word is bound". base-defs.reb uses that
--- note to hang six generated words off REFLECT's own context without
--- note naming the context at all.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
w: bind 'a o
mold bind 'a w
--- result
"a"

--- id contexts/a-word-bound-that-way-reaches-the-same-value
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
w: bind 'a o
get bind 'a w
--- result
1

--- id contexts/appending-to-an-object-adds-a-field
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note An object is not a series, so APPEND on one means "extend it"
--- note rather than "put this at the end". The block is read as
--- note set-word and value pairs. Rebol's own REGISTER-CODEC adds to
--- note system/codecs this way.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
append o [b: 2]
o/b
--- result
2

--- id contexts/the-field-shows-up-in-the-words
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
append o [b: 2]
mold words-of o
--- result
"[a b]"

--- id contexts/the-set-word-may-be-computed
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the point: the field name is not in the source, so an
--- note object can gain a field decided at run time.
--- code
p: make object! [a: 1]
append p reduce [to set-word! 'c 9]
p/c
--- result
9

--- id contexts/converting-a-word-to-a-set-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The word family converts within itself, which is how code
--- note builds an assignment it did not spell out.
--- code
mold to set-word! 'c
--- result
"c:"

--- id contexts/and-back-again
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to word! first [c:]
--- result
"c"

--- id contexts/a-word-to-a-lit-word
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to lit-word! 'c
--- result
"'c"

--- id contexts/putting-a-field-into-an-object
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note PUT answers the value rather than the thing it put it in,
--- note which makes it awkward in a chain and is worth knowing before
--- note you write one.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
put o 'b 2
--- result
2

--- id contexts/and-the-field-is-there-afterwards
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1]
put o 'b 2
o/b
--- result
2

--- id contexts/putting-a-key-into-a-map
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
m: make map! []
put m 'k 5
m/k
--- result
5

--- id contexts/putting-into-a-block-replaces-the-value-after-the-key
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A block is read as key and value pairs here, the way SELECT
--- note reads one.
--- code
b: [a 1]
put b 'a 9
mold b
--- result
"[a 9]"

--- id contexts/walking-an-objects-words
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note FOREACH over an object walks its words, so an object can be
--- note inspected without asking for WORDS-OF first.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1 b: 2]
mold collect [foreach w o [keep w]]
--- result
"[a b]"

--- id contexts/walking-an-objects-words-and-values-together
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Two words in the loop's spec take a word and its value each
--- note time round, the way they take pairs from a block.
--- code
o: make object! [a: 1 b: 2]
mold collect [foreach [k v] o [keep reduce [k v]]]
--- result
"[a 1 b 2]"
